**Aging and waiting lists.** The clearest public demand signal is the **CO Waiver registry: 23,461 people at the end of FY2025**, up 5% (1,112 people) from FY2024 [S41]. In FY2025 the waiver was authorized for **6,348 participants, but only 4,253 slots were filled**. Another 750 were reserved and 1,345 were unfilled [S41]. Registry growth limits how fast waiver residents arrive. Unfilled slots mean MDH is short on processing capacity, not on demand. A nursing-home resident whose stay Medicaid has paid for 30+ days can apply **without waiting on the registry** [S45]. That makes nursing-home transitions the fastest waiver referral source.

**Supply by county.** OHCQ's directory showed the most programs in Baltimore City (402), Prince George's (304), Baltimore County (263) and Montgomery (238) [S32]. Montgomery had the most beds (4,961) and the most large facilities (40 over 16 beds) [S32]. Small homes are dense in Baltimore City and Prince George's, where competition for the same referrals is highest [S32].

**Unlicensed competition.** OHCQ receives 8–10 complaints a month about unlicensed homes. It found 13 unlicensed programs in FY2025 and 10 in July–December 2025 [S33]. A licensed home can market its OHCQ license and survey record against them.

**Referral sources:**
- hospital and nursing-home discharge planners;
- CO Waiver case managers (they visit waiver residents quarterly) [S37];
- Maryland Access Point (844-627-5465), the waiver registry's front door [S45];
- local Area Agencies on Aging, which run SALS [S46];
- hospice agencies (hospice residents may be admitted above normal limits) [S20];
- placement agencies.

Operators must give prospective residents the **Uniform Disclosure Statement** as part of marketing [S1].
